<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devaraja</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devaraja ('god-king') was a religious and political concept associating the ruler with a divine power, usually <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. It was central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> from its foundation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a>, legitimising royal authority and the building of state temples such as those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>. Comparable ideas of sacral kingship also shaped governance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devaraja.jpg" alt="Devaraja" width="320" height="631" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harihara_Majapahit_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its kings underwent a process of Indianisation to consolidate and magnify their rule, adopting the <b>devaraja</b> idea of the god-king, Sanskrit as the language of the gods and the script of the Pallavas and Chalukyas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundation is traditionally dated to 802 CE, when Jayavarman II underwent a consecration ritual on Mount Mahendraparvata, the Phnom Kulen plateau, proclaiming himself a universal monarch (chakravartin), inaugurating the cult of the <b>devaraja</b> and symbolically freeing the land from the overlordship of “Java”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century real power passed to the ministers: from the 1730s the dalavayi <b>Devaraja</b> and his brother Nanjaraja dominated the court, and from 1761 Hyder Ali took control of the government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khmer prince Jayavarman II spent years at the Shailendra court before returning around 790 to rule as <b>devaraja</b> and proclaim independence from Java in 802, founding the Angkor era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At its head stood a king whom the cult of the <b>devaraja</b>, the “god-king”, identified with Shiva or Vishnu, or under Jayavarman VII (r. 1181–1219) with the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, and whose temples, Angkor Wat and the Bayon, celebrated his divine rule on earth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
